## 6. Dependency rule

Feature modules may require shared capabilities, but not another commercial module's UI or controller. Example: pickup ordering uses `catalog` + `orders`; it does not call `qr-menu`. QR is a presentation/channel over the same catalog. WhatsApp ordering calls the same order application service as web pickup. Catering uses `lead-records` but not the general CRM campaign UI.

Each module declares:
- required capabilities;
- optional integrations;
- routes/pages it contributes;
- data tables it owns;
- event types it publishes/consumes;
- tenant entitlement key;
- health checks and setup checklist;
- disable/deletion behavior.

## 6. Shared `PosAdapter` contract

```ts
interface PosAdapter {
  testConnection(input: ConnectionConfig): Promise<ConnectionTestResult>;
  getCapabilities(input: ConnectionConfig): Promise<PosCapabilityMap>;
  fetchProducts(input: ConnectionConfig, cursor?: string): Promise<Page<NormalizedProduct>>;
  fetchSales(input: ConnectionConfig, range: DateRange, cursor?: string): Promise<Page<NormalizedSale>>;
  createExternalOrder?(input: ConnectionConfig, order: PosOrderPayload): Promise<PosOrderResult>;
  getOrderStatus?(input: ConnectionConfig, externalOrderId: string): Promise<NormalizedPosOrderStatus>;
  verifyWebhook?(request: PosWebhookRequest): Promise<NormalizedPosEvent>;
}
```

Optional methods are absent if unsupported; do not implement as fake success. Feature module checks recorded capability before calling method.

## 3. Pricing engine

Use deterministic versioned rules, not an LLM. Inputs include guest count, menu/package, service hours, staff roles/count, setup/cleanup, equipment, travel/venue, optional beverages, add-ons, minimum spend and owner-defined seasonal/date multipliers.

Example rule model:
- package fixed price per guest;
- staff charge = roles × estimated hours × configured rate;
- setup/transport/equipment fixed or parameterized fee;
- optional items/contingency configured by owner;
- minimum guest count/minimum event price;
- owner-defined tax display rule reviewed locally;
- currency CLP, integer pesos.

Every price/rule set has a version. Each quote stores a full pricing snapshot; later price edits do not mutate already issued quotes.

## 5. Prototype feedback test

Give each owner/staff user tasks without explaining where to click. Observe completion, hesitation and mistaken assumptions. Ask what they'd remove, what is a blocker, what they'd pay for and what they'd expect to connect to their POS. Capture priority as:
- `MUST_BUY`: explicitly blocks purchase;
- `CORE_VALUE`: materially improves outcome;
- `LATER`: nice-to-have;
- `NOT_NEEDED`: user rejects/unneeded;
- `UNKNOWN`: insufficient evidence.

Do not treat a feature mentioned by one participant as a universal requirement.

## 6. Pilot agreement checklist

A lightweight written pilot agreement should define:
- chosen module/workflow and exclusions;
- success metric and baseline;
- restaurant owner and operational champion;
- start/end dates and whether paid/discounted;
- data access and provider authorizations;
- who handles support and staff training;
- manual work that founders will do during pilot;
- data retention/export and privacy notice;
- what happens if provider integration is blocked;
- permission for case study/testimonial, separate from service agreement.

## 7. Concierge pilot options

Depending on workflow, founders can manually:
- import menu CSV and check product mapping;
- enter catering package rules and inspect generated quote;
- reconcile POS export daily rather than integrate first;
- manually trigger customer win-back list after consent review;
- watch test orders and manually confirm external POS submission;
- use payment-at-pickup rather than online checkout if acceptable.

Do not manually fake payment status, bypass consent, store raw card data or claim a POS action occurred if it did not. Manual operations should produce a log of every intervention and time spent.

## 8. Pilot success and stop conditions

Success is an observable improvement, not “the UI looks good.” Example metrics: time to produce/send a quote, staff interventions per order, orders successfully fulfilled, fewer duplicate/manual entries, repeat purchase attributed to campaign. Agree exact metric with owner.

Stop/pivot if:
- no owner will commit time/data/access;
- nobody identifies meaningful willingness to pay;
- integration blocks the only desired value and no manual fallback is acceptable;
- custom setup/support per restaurant is too high to repeat;
- product increases operational risk in restaurant peak hours.

## 2. Data inventory and purposes

Maintain a table/record of:
- data category, purpose, source and lawful basis/consent as applicable;
- controller/processor roles and subprocessors;
- where data is hosted and transferred;
- retention period and deletion/archive behavior;
- customer rights workflow;
- whether data is required operationally or optional marketing data.

Separate order fulfilment, transactional notifications, loyalty program operation, analytics and marketing purposes. Never make marketing consent a precondition for buying food or requesting a catering quote unless local legal advice confirms a necessary basis.

## 3. Payment adapter interface

```ts
interface PaymentAdapter {
  createCheckout(input: CreateCheckoutInput): Promise<CheckoutSession>;
  verifyWebhook(input: ProviderWebhookRequest): Promise<VerifiedPaymentEvent>;
  getPaymentStatus(input: GetPaymentStatusInput): Promise<NormalizedPaymentStatus>;
  requestRefund?(input: RefundInput): Promise<RefundResult>;
  createPaymentLink?(input: CreatePaymentLinkInput): Promise<PaymentLinkResult>;
}
```

Adapter normalizes statuses. It must keep provider reference, transaction amount, currency and event ID. Every create attempt uses provider-supported idempotency where documented. Provider credentials never reach the client.
